mpp_logicalrep -- logical replication apply into an MPP target
==============================================================

Stock logical replication replays a stream in one process, which writes
the rows itself.  On Cloudberry that process may not write them: every
user table is spread over the segments, and only a segment can write its
own part.  This module is the receiving end built the other way round.
The process that reads the stream never touches a table; it drives an
ordinary coordinator session, and the apply operators run on gangs:

    source coordinator stream --> apply driver (one worker per subscription)
    source segment streams    --> reader gang
                                       |
                                       v
                              Explicit Redistribute Motion
                                       |
                                       v
                                  writer gang --> tables

The driver is a background worker on the coordinator.  It reads the
source coordinator's stream, asks the reader gang for the pieces of each
committed distributed transaction, batches the ready ones, and applies
each batch in a single distributed transaction of the target, recording
its stream position in that same transaction.  A crash therefore
replays from a position that is exactly as old as the data.


Reassembling a distributed transaction
--------------------------------------

A distributed transaction on the source is not one stream.  It leaves a
piece of WAL on each participating segment plus a commit record on the
coordinator, and the pieces have nothing in common that a receiver can
see.  Two things from the server make them one transaction again; both
are described in the commits that precede this one.

First, commit order and identity.  The coordinator's distributed commit
record carries the gxid and the participating segments, and pgoutput
emits it as the non-transactional message "cbdb.dcommit" under the
cbdb_events option.  Each segment's own stream carries the same gxid,
as "cbdb.gxid" under cbdb_gxid.  The driver takes its commit order from
the coordinator's events and matches each one against what the readers
have.

Second, what to wait for.  Waiting for a segment that never wrote stalls
the stream until a timeout, and a distributed transaction is dispatched
to many more segments than it writes on.  Each segment reports at
PREPARE whether it holds an xid, so the event usually names the exact
write set and is marked so; the driver then waits for all of them,
because each really does have the transaction.  When the report is
incomplete the event names the wider dispatch set instead, and the
driver waits on it with much shorter patience.


Initial synchronisation
-----------------------

Tables are copied segment to segment, not through the coordinator: each
source segment sends its own rows to a helper process on a target
segment (copyhelper/, driven by COPY ... ON SEGMENT), and the
redistribution to the right target segment is done by the Motion of the
enclosing INSERT ... SELECT.

The copy is taken under a cut point: a target LSN and a distributed
snapshot of the source.  A decoded transaction whose gxid is visible
under that snapshot is already in the copy and is dropped rather than
applied, which is what makes the copy and the stream meet exactly once.

ALTER SUBSCRIPTION ... REFRESH PUBLICATION adds tables to a running
subscription the same way: their changes are held aside, the tables are
copied under a cut point of their own while everything else keeps
streaming, the held changes are replayed, and only then do they go live.


Installation
------------

The library must be preloaded.  It registers the launcher, and it
registers the custom scan methods of the apply plans, which any backend
may have to deserialize when a plan is dispatched to it:

    shared_preload_libraries = 'mpp_logicalrep'

There is no CREATE EXTENSION step.  The catalog objects are installed by
initdb, from cdb_init.d, because they have to be coordinator-only tables
like the information_schema ones: the driver writes them in the same
distributed transaction as the batch it applied and reads them back on
the coordinator.  A table made by an ordinary CREATE TABLE would be
distributed, its rows would live on the segments, and the driver's own
reads would not find them.  An existing cluster therefore needs the
objects installed by hand, or a new initdb.

The same applies to a cluster whose objects were installed by an earlier
version of this module.  The script creates, it does not upgrade, so
anything added since has to be added by hand -- in every database that
has the objects, and in template1 so that new databases get it.  As of
this version that is the subscription option column

    SET allow_system_table_mods = on;
    ALTER TABLE pg_catalog.gp_mpp_subscription
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS unsupported_tables text
        NOT NULL DEFAULT 'error';

-- without which the apply worker cannot read the subscription's settings
and disables the subscription saying which column is missing -- and the
two statistics functions and the two views that read them, which are the
last section of the script and can be run on their own:

    SET allow_system_table_mods = on;
    SET search_path = pg_catalog;
    \i .../cdb_init.d/mpp_logicalrep_cdbinit.sql   -- from CREATE FUNCTION
                                                   -- gp_stat_get_mpp_subscription

The library has to be the new one before either is run: CREATE FUNCTION
looks the C symbols up in the library the postmaster already has open, so
the cluster is restarted first.

The source cluster needs nothing from this module.


Usage
-----

On the source, a publication and a replication slot on every primary
segment and on the coordinator, under one name:

    CREATE PUBLICATION p FOR TABLE ...;
    SELECT gp_segment_id, slot_name
      FROM gp_create_logical_replication_slot('sub_s', 'pgoutput',
                                              false, true)
     ORDER BY 1;

On the target, ordinary syntax -- a loaded module means the subscription
is applied by it:

    CREATE SUBSCRIPTION s
        CONNECTION 'host=... port=... dbname=...'
        PUBLICATION p
        WITH (slot_name = 'sub_s');

slot_name is the one option that must be given: it names the slots made
above, which the readers stream from, and nothing can guess it.
WITH (copy_data = false) declares the tables already in sync, so the
driver starts streaming without copying, and WITH (enabled = false)
creates the subscription without starting it.
WITH (unsupported_tables = 'snapshot') accepts a published table whose
changes cannot be decoded (below); all three can also be changed later
with ALTER SUBSCRIPTION ... SET.

The statement is rewritten in ProcessUtility_hook: the subscription is
recorded in pg_catalog.gp_mpp_subscription, and connect = false is added
so that the pg_subscription row stays disabled.  That is what keeps the
server's own launcher from starting a stock apply worker, which on
Cloudberry would write the rows on the coordinator, where no user table
has any.  ENABLE, DISABLE, REFRESH PUBLICATION and DROP SUBSCRIPTION are
intercepted the same way, so they read as usual;
gp_mpp_subscription.enabled, not pg_subscription.subenabled, is what
says a subscription is running.

Two options are therefore not the user's to give, and are refused with a
reason rather than silently overridden:

    connect          the module sets it; a subscription dumped by
                     pg_dump carries "connect = false" and has to have
                     it removed, or be dumped with --no-subscriptions
    create_slot      the slots are cluster-wide and only
                     gp_create_logical_replication_slot() can make them

WITH (mpp) is still accepted and means nothing extra.  WITH (mpp = false)
is the way to ask for a subscription of the server's own kind, which the
module then leaves entirely alone.

DROP SUBSCRIPTION does not touch the publisher: the slots were not the
module's to create and are not its to drop, which is also what lets the
statement run in a transaction block.  Drop them on the publisher with
gp_drop_replication_slot('sub_s') once nothing needs them -- a forgotten
set of slots holds down the WAL of every primary segment there.


Requirements on the tables
--------------------------

Two conditions are checked per table when it is synchronised, and
reported as warnings rather than errors so that a subscription covering
many tables still starts.

The target's distribution key must be covered by the source's replica
identity.  An UPDATE that changes a target distribution column has to
move the row between segments, which apply does as DELETE + INSERT, and
that needs the whole old key.  Without it the UPDATE is refused.

The source's distribution key must be covered by the target's replica
identity.  Otherwise two rows that lived on different source segments
can collide on one target row, and the DELETE + INSERT pair for one of
them lands out of order.

Only heap tables can be published.  Append-optimised tables (ao_row,
ao_column) and PAX have no logical decoding routine on the publisher, so
nothing they do ever reaches the stream: such a table would be copied by
the initial synchronisation and then never change again, without an
error anywhere.  It is therefore refused, by name and by access method,
when the subscription takes its table list -- which is when the
subscription starts, and, for a table added later, in the ALTER
SUBSCRIPTION ... REFRESH PUBLICATION itself:

    unsupported_tables = 'error'      refuse (the default)
    unsupported_tables = 'snapshot'   copy it once, as of the cut point,
                                      and stop there

A snapshot table is recorded in gp_subscription_rel with state 's' and
is never looked at again; the setting is read when a table list is
taken, so changing it afterwards does not revisit tables already taken
on.  Append-optimised tables on the TARGET are fully supported -- it is
decoding on the publisher that they have no part in.


Configuration parameters
------------------------

mpp_logicalrep.batch_max_txns (integer, default 64)
mpp_logicalrep.batch_max_bytes (integer, default 64MB)
mpp_logicalrep.batch_max_latency_ms (integer, default 200ms)
    When a batch is applied: whichever comes first.

mpp_logicalrep.event_wait_ms (integer, default 1s)
    How long one drain waits for the transaction of a commit event.

mpp_logicalrep.idle_drain_ms (integer, default 2s)
    How often the readers are polled while the source is idle.

mpp_logicalrep.reader_mem (integer, default 64MB)
    Memory a reader may use to buffer changes before spilling to disk,
    per subscription.

mpp_logicalrep.apply_max_retries (integer, default 10)
mpp_logicalrep.apply_error_action (enum, default disable)
    What to do with an error that keeps coming back: disable the
    subscription after that many attempts, or retry for ever.  Errors
    that can pass on their own -- a lost connection, a reset gang, a
    deadlock -- are always retried and reset the count.

mpp_logicalrep.slot_loss_action (enum, default reinit)
    What to do when the source's slots are gone, which a segment
    failover does: resynchronise the subscription from scratch, or
    disable it and leave it to an operator.

mpp_logicalrep.copy_port_lo, mpp_logicalrep.copy_port_hi (integer,
default 42000 and 42999)
mpp_logicalrep.copy_crc (boolean, default on)
    The copy transport's listen range, and whether to checksum each of
    its frames.

mpp_logicalrep.max_workers (integer, default 8, requires restart)
    How many subscriptions may have a worker at once; it also sizes the
    shared-memory slots the workers publish their statistics in.

mpp_logicalrep.launcher_naptime_ms (integer, default 5s)
    How long the launcher sleeps between scans.  CREATE SUBSCRIPTION and
    ALTER SUBSCRIPTION ... ENABLE wake it when they commit, so this is
    the delay only for a worker that has to be started for some other
    reason -- one that exited, say.


Monitoring
----------

gp_stat_mpp_subscription has a row per subscription this module owns,
running or not: a subscription that stopped is the one an operator comes
looking for, so the registry drives the view and the live columns are
simply null.

    phase             starting, initial sync, streaming, table sync,
                      stopping, or "not running"
    received_lsn      how far the source coordinator's WAL has got, as
                      it last said
    applied_lsn       the position of the last committed batch
    apply_lag_bytes   the difference.  It does not fall to zero on an
                      idle subscription: the source coordinator writes
                      WAL of its own that this target has no batch to
                      commit against.
    tables, tables_ready, tables_syncing, tables_snapshot
                      the state rows of gp_subscription_rel
    awaited_txns      seen on the coordinator stream, not yet complete
                      on every segment it wrote on
    deferred_txns     held back because they touched a table still
                      being copied
    batch_txns, batch_bytes
                      the batch being filled right now
    buffered_txns, buffered_bytes
                      what the readers are still holding, memory and
                      spill together, summed over the source segments
    applied_txns      deduplication triples still above the flush point
    events, batches, txns_applied, local_txns_applied, rows_applied,
    recoveries, stuck_errors
                      counters since this worker started; a restart
                      resets them and nothing is persisted

gp_stat_mpp_subscription_segments breaks the per-segment part out, one
row per source segment: where its reader has read to, where a replay
would start, and what it is holding.  The positions come from the state
table when no worker is running, which is where the last committed batch
left them, so the view says something useful about a subscription that
has stopped.

Both read one shared-memory slot per worker, so they cost nothing and
can be polled.


Files
-----

mpp_logicalrep.c           settings, the subscription DDL, the launcher
mpp_apply_worker.c         the driver: events, batching, the assembly
logicalrep_apply_driver.c  the target-neutral loop
logicalrep_sink.h          the sink/assembly contract the loop is
                           written against
mpp_sink_cbdb.c            the Cloudberry sink: apply a batch, record
                           progress
mpp_source.c, mpp_reader.c the source streams and the reader gang
mpp_apply_plan.c           the apply plans
mpp_apply_sql.c            their SQL entry points
mpp_route.c, mpp_writer.c  routing to a segment, and writing there
mpp_tablesync.c            initial synchronisation, REFRESH PUBLICATION
copyhelper/                the segment-to-segment copy transport
test/                      unit tests of the apply loop, against a mock
                           sink and no server
test/cluster/              tests that drive real clusters, and how to
                           set the clusters up (see its own README)

The loop in logicalrep_apply_driver.c knows about units of work,
progress and errors, and nothing about gangs or motions; everything
Cloudberry-specific is behind logicalrep_sink.h.  That is what lets the
loop be tested without a cluster, and what a different target would
implement.


Limitations
-----------

Append-optimized and PAX tables work as targets but not as sources: on
Cloudberry they produce no logical decoding output at all.
